  "body": " A local criterion for adjoint generation  Local generation   The final step of the proof is a local criterion for generation of at a closed point . Its hypotheses compare the coefficients of a rational divisor with log discrepancies, both on a reduced divisor over and on the other prime divisors meeting it. Global nefness and bigness give vanishing, while the coefficient comparisons give a section on the reduced divisor that can be lifted.    Vanishing and descent  We use Kawamata–Viehweg vanishing, proved in and , in the rounding form of .   Kawamata–Viehweg vanishing   Let be smooth and projective over . Let be an effective rational divisor with simple normal crossing support and coefficients in , and let be an integral divisor. If is nef and big, then    To obtain this statement from the rounding form, take . The support of its fractional part is simple normal crossing and . In particular, if is an integral divisor, is an effective rational divisor with simple normal crossing support, and is nef and big, then For this application the boundary is , and the required nef and big divisor is .    Let be a proper birational morphism of smooth varieties, let be a line bundle on , and let be an integral divisor on whose positive part is exceptional over . A section of defines a regular section of on .  Suppose that a prime divisor on maps to a point , that the coefficient of on is zero, and that the section is nonzero generically on . Then its descended section is nonzero at .    Regard the section as a rational section of in the common function field. On each nonexceptional prime of , the order of its pullback is at least the negative of the coefficient of , and this coefficient is nonpositive. Every prime divisor on has a nonexceptional strict transform on , so the rational section has no pole along any prime of . Normality of extends it from codimension one to a regular section everywhere.  At , the coefficient of is zero, so nonvanishing at its generic point means that the pullback of the descended section has order zero. A local function vanishing at has positive order along every prime centered at . In a local frame of near , the coefficient of the descended section therefore cannot vanish at .      The local generation criterion  Generation criterion  Fujita's argument in uses equality on a divisor over and strict discrepancy inequalities on the components meeting it. Kawamata–Viehweg vanishing lifts a section from that divisor, and a correction whose positive part is exceptional permits descent. The following lemma gives this argument for a possibly reducible reduced divisor .    Let be a smooth projective variety over of dimension , let be an ample line bundle, and let be a closed point. Let be a projective birational morphism with smooth, and let be an effective rational divisor with SNC support. For a prime divisor on , write Assume that is nef and big. Suppose that is a nonempty effective reduced divisor on such that every component of satisfies and ; every other prime divisor on meeting satisfies .  Then there is a section with , where .    Define the integral Cartier divisor Since the fractional part is an effective rational SNC divisor with coefficients in , The right side is nef and big by hypothesis. Kawamata–Viehweg vanishing in Theorem  , equivalently the rounding formulation of , therefore gives . Since is smooth, is Cartier. Its restriction sequence is The vanishing gives a surjection  To construct a section on , compare with the pullback of : Smoothness of and implies that every is a positive integer. For a component of , the coefficient of is , and the first hypothesis gives . Hence For any other prime meeting , the second hypothesis gives . Its coefficient in is zero, so its coefficient in is nonnegative. A prime outside and the exceptional locus of has coefficient zero in : its coefficient in is zero, and it cannot be a component of because every component of has positive coefficient in .  It follows that is effective on a neighborhood of and that no component of occurs in its support. More explicitly, every negative component of is disjoint from . Remove the supports of these finitely many components to obtain an open neighborhood of on which is effective. This local effectivity permits restriction of the canonical rational section of to .  Every component of maps to , and is reduced. Therefore factors scheme-theoretically through the reduced point . To check this, take a local function in the maximal ideal of . Its pullback restricts to zero on each irreducible component of and hence to zero on , because the intersection of the minimal prime ideals of a reduced ring is zero. It follows that Choose a nonzero vector in the one-dimensional fiber . Under this isomorphism it gives a constant section of . Multiply that constant section by the restriction to of the canonical rational section of . The canonical section is regular on the neighborhood just constructed and is nonzero at the generic point of each component of , because the coefficient of there is zero. The product is a global section of nonzero at the generic point of every component. This construction uses effectivity of only near , since its negative components elsewhere are disjoint from .  Lift this section to using  . View it as a section of . On any nonexceptional prime outside , the coefficient of is zero, and therefore On a component of , the coefficient of is zero. Thus the positive part of is exceptional over . The divisor is nonempty, and each of its components maps to , has coefficient zero in , and has nonzero at its generic point. Lemma  gives a regular section with .  The proof includes . A component of can then be the nonexceptional point itself. Its coefficient in is still zero, the restriction sequence is the sequence for this effective Cartier divisor on the smooth curve, and the same descent argument gives .     "
