  "body": " The exponential functional  Exponential functional   We continue to fix and now let both the basis and the monomial order vary. For a positive integer with , an ordered basis of , a nonzero monomial test , and , define The infimum is over all ordered bases and all allowed nonzero tests whose closed center contains . Positive rescaling is allowed. Rational approximation of the weights on their fixed positive support preserves the center and approximates all section orders and the discrepancy. Restricting the infimum to positive multiples of prime-divisor orders therefore does not change its value.  To prove attainment, we need discrepancy bounds that are uniform for sufficiently large and for near . We also need a single basis on which to take a limit of monomial weights. Ordinary jets give the discrepancy bounds, and closed loci on the complete flag variety allow us to replace the varying bases by one fixed basis.    Order and discrepancy bounds  Uniform bounds    If , then every nonzero section satisfies In particular, for , where is the constant of Lemma  .    For a local equation of an effective divisor at , let . If , we first prove Choose a general coordinate direction in which the initial homogeneous form is nonzero. Weierstrass preparation writes , on a sufficiently small polydisc, as a unit times a monic polynomial of degree in that coordinate. With the other coordinates fixed, factor this polynomial as . The roots remain uniformly bounded as the complementary coordinates vary.  For , Hölder's inequality gives The integrals on the right are uniformly finite: , and after translation by the root each integration domain lies inside a fixed disc centered at the origin. The absolute value of the unit factor is bounded above and away from zero. Fubini's theorem then gives local integrability of . Letting tend to proves the threshold bound. If , is a unit near and the threshold is infinite.  For a divisorial order whose closed center contains , the valuative threshold formula consequently gives . This includes orders along positive-dimensional centers containing . For the corresponding estimate at the generic center of a valuation, see also . Rational approximation on a fixed monomial support proves the same inequality for all our tests. Applying proves the final bound.      Fix . There are a constant and an integer such that for and , The constants do not depend on or in these ranges.    Choose such that is very ample. Take sections of for which and are regular parameters at . For each residue class modulo , choose a sufficiently large fixed integer in that class such that is globally generated. Choose nonzero at . Such exist by ampleness; see . For in class , write . For sufficiently large, .  For every multi-index with , the section has, in a frame of , germ equal to a common unit times . Let The classes of these sections for are linearly independent in the quotient by sections of ordinary order greater than . Indeed, multiplication by the common unit preserves ordinary order, and a nonzero linear combination of the monomials has total degree at most . There are such classes.  Because the choices are fixed and finite in number, uniformly in . Thus is at least a fixed positive multiple of for all large . Comparing with the Hilbert asymptotic yields for a fixed .      Suppose that is not generated at . There are constants , , , , and an integer such that, for every and every , Every admissible pair with and satisfies All constants are independent of and in the stated ranges.    By Proposition  , choose such that for all sufficiently large , Choose sufficiently close to that . Take a basis with distinct ordinary initials and use , where is a scaling factor. Here is the order of the exceptional divisor of the point blowup, with discrepancy ; in dimension one it is the order at the point. Set . The objective is Its linear coefficient at is at most . The numbers are uniformly bounded by and . The remainder after the linear term is therefore uniformly . One fixed sufficiently small gives an upper bound , with fixed , for every and under consideration.  Apply Lemma  for this , and let . By Lemma  , The subspace on the left consequently has codimension at least . At least that many vectors of any basis lie outside it, since the vectors inside are linearly independent and their number is at most the dimension of the subspace. The basis need not be adapted to . Each vector outside contributes at least to the exponential sum, so This gives the lower bound in , with , and bounds above on the sublevel set .  Finally, and imply , and hence . Choose so small that . Every pair in the stated sublevel set has . Enlarge the upper bound if necessary so that . Taking large enough for both the ordinary-order estimate and the jet estimate completes the proof.     "
