  "body": "  Fix with and rational numbers . The set of points for which an ordered basis and a prime-divisor order with satisfy is Zariski closed. More precisely, there is a closed incidence locus in , where is the complete flag variety of , recording realization of these inequalities by bases adapted to the given flag.    A flag is a chain of subspaces with . Let be the base ideal of , that is, the image of the evaluation morphism . A basis is adapted to this flag if is spanned by its first vectors.  For a decreasing list , for an adapted basis is equivalent to In one direction, each of the first vectors has value at least , so every element of does. Conversely, the inequality for the base ideal of applies to its basis vector . Thus if the flag inequalities hold, every basis adapted to that flag satisfies the section inequalities.  Assume at least one is positive. Choose a positive integer such that is an integer for each positive entry, and form Since the value of a sum of ideals is the minimum of their values and the value of a power multiplies by its exponent, Therefore is equivalent to . There exists a prime-divisor order satisfying these inequalities whose closed center contains if and only if The forward implication is the valuative threshold inequality. The reverse implication follows because the local threshold is computed by a prime component on a log resolution, whose image contains .  The universal subbundles on give algebraic families of generators of the on . Taking the indicated powers and sum gives an algebraic family of generators of . Lemma  shows that the incidence locus defined by is closed. Since the flag variety is projective, projection of this incidence locus to is closed. If every is zero, the inequalities impose no restriction, and both loci are the whole parameter spaces.   "
